From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Henrik Klee <henrik@goat-code.com>
Date: Fri, 13 Nov 2026 06:40:00 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] soc: apple: aop: Wait for the firmware's hello at start

The AOP driver loads its firmware and starts the coprocessor itself,
then called apple_rtkit_wake(), which first sends the firmware an IOP
power state request: the call for firmware that is already running and
asleep. A firmware that was just started reaches its mailbox only after
it has initialised, so the request can arrive first. On the iPad 6 the
AOP crashed 1 ms after start in about one boot in six ("co-processor
has crashed", then "firmware did not boot: -62"), leaving the device
without accelerometer and rotation.

Use apple_rtkit_boot(), which waits for the firmware's hello, endpoint
map and power state report, as the Apple NVMe and the T8010 GPU
(pvr_apple) drivers do for a coprocessor they start from scratch.

Assisted-by: LLM
---
diff --git a/drivers/soc/apple/aop.c b/drivers/soc/apple/aop.c
index 6d94720..5c23317 100644
--- a/drivers/soc/apple/aop.c
+++ b/drivers/soc/apple/aop.c
@@ -1054,7 +1054,14 @@ static int apple_aop_probe(struct platform_device *pdev)
 	       aop->akf + APPLE_AOP_CPU_CONTROL);
 	writel(1, aop->control + APPLE_AOP_CONTROL_AUTO_BOOT);
 
-	ret = apple_rtkit_wake(aop->rtk);
+	/*
+	 * The firmware was just loaded and starts from scratch: wait for its
+	 * hello and its own power state report, as for any freshly started
+	 * coprocessor. Asking for a power state first (apple_rtkit_wake(), for
+	 * firmware that is already running) reaches it before its mailbox is
+	 * up.
+	 */
+	ret = apple_rtkit_boot(aop->rtk);
 	if (ret) {
 		dev_err(dev, "firmware did not boot: %d\n", ret);
 		goto err_halt;
-- 
2.54.0
